<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Via Augusta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Via Augusta was the longest Roman road in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, running about 1,500 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a> and Valentia and then inland to Corduba and Gades. Rebuilt under Augustus, after whom it was named, on the line of older routes, it shaped the main travel routes of eastern and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/via-augusta.jpg" alt="Via Augusta" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Year of the dragon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spain.Catalonia.Roda.de.Bara.Arc.Bera.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Augustus stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> between 16 and 13 BCE, as part of a reorganisation of its provinces that aimed at new cities, roads to connect them and land for veteran soldiers. The model was the network of roads that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa</a> had laid out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in 20–19 BCE. Augustus ordered the rebuilding of the older Via Herculea, which ran from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a>, and its extension to Gades, using what remained of roads of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>. The work was carried out between 8 and 2 BCE, and many milestones bearing his name have been found along it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The historian Pierre Sillières, who has supervised excavations to trace its route, describes it as more a system of roads than a single road. It ran from the Coll de Pertús pass in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> south and south-west to the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Baetis</a>, where an arch, the Ianus Augusti, stood on the border between Baetica and Tarraconensis, and from which the milestones to Gades were numbered. It was usually four to six metres wide, with sections of ten or fourteen metres, and pavements only near cities. Continuing the Via Julia Augusta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-aemilia">Via Aemilia</a> and Via Flaminia, it formed part of a chain of roads joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to the Atlantic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a> as a military road, it served mainly for general communication and trade, and helped cities such as Valentia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucentum">Lucentum</a>, Saetabis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-alcudia-elche">Ilici</a> to grow. In 90 CE Domitian restored a dilapidated stretch near Corduba. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> still used it in the 10th century, calling it al-Racif, and parts of it served traffic until they were paved in the 1920s. The N-340 road and the A-7 highway follow much of its course, and remains include the Arc de Berà north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarragona">Tarragona</a>, the Alcantarilla bridge near Utrera and paving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>'s Almoina museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Via Augusta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its roads, above all the <b>Via Augusta</b>, which ran down the Mediterranean coast from the Pyrenees, and the network linking the Ebro valley with the Meseta and Galicia, shaped the routes of Spain for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
